I fixed all three issues from the architect's review in `linearize.cljc` and added tests for each. The JVM tests for the linearizer pass (13 tests, 414 assertions), and so does the full Node test suite (1290 tests, 0 failures). I couldn't run the Dart tests: `clojure -M:cljd test` needs approval that this session can't give, so run it yourself before merging. Nothing is committed.

1. **[P1] Literal maps no longer get rejected.** `lower-ast` used to search every nested map for an unsupported node type, including inside literal values. It now walks only real child nodes (lambda body, operator and operands, `if` branches, stream sources, the resume value) and treats literal values as plain data. New test `literals-are-opaque-data`: literals shaped like `{:type :vm/store-update}` or `{:type :yin/macro-expand}` now lower, and `lower-ast` gives the same result as `lower` on the datoms.

2. **[P2] Custom `:id-start` is now checked.** `lower` throws if `:id-start` isn't a negative integer, or if any id it would use (the segment id down through the last instruction id) matches an entity in the input. The error lists the colliding ids. IDs are handed out by counting down from the start, so they are always distinct. New test `id-start-is-validated-over-the-whole-range` covers a collision with the root, the segment id itself colliding, the invalid values `1`, `0`, `-1.5` and `:k`, and a clear range that is accepted.

3. **[P1] Host functions are kept out of code datoms.** Every data operand is now checked all the way down (literal values, stored values, params, names, keys and so on); only jump and body labels are skipped. Allowed values are nil, booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, and collections of those. Anything else throws with `{:node <source AST eid>, :attr, :value}`. Primitives are still referenced by symbol through `:var`. New test `host-values-are-rejected-naming-the-node` covers `{:nested [inc]}` in a literal, `#{inc}` in a stored value, and `+` compiling to a `:var` name.

The allowed-value check is deliberately strict, so a `\a` char, a UUID or an `#inst` inside a literal is now rejected too. The current test programs don't use any, but loosening the check would be a one-line change if a program needs them.
